{"line":41,"status":"PASS","pdf_page":"UNCHECKED","source":"Let $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ be independent real random variables with densities bounded by $K$, and let $Q=X^{T}AX+b^{T}X+c$ with $A$ real symmetric. We prove that if the fifth singular value of $A$ is at least $s>0$, then $Q$ has a density bounded by $270\\,K^2/s$, uniformly in $n$, $b$, $c$, the rest of the spectrum of $A$ and the laws of the $X_i$. The proof combines a decay estimate for the characteristic function of $Q$ in terms of the crossing volumes of an off-diagonal block of $A$, a random sign argument that, in the absence of a pivot, turns $2m-1$ large eigenvalue moduli into $m$ large singular values of such a block for $m\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, and conditioning on one or two coordinates when $A$ has a large diagonal entry or a large principal $2\\times2$ minor.","hypotheses":"Printed hypotheses only; no added iid/moment/support/smoothness assumption.","derivation":"Abstract matches repaired theorem; random-sign claim now explicitly requires absence of a pivot and m in {1,2,3}.","dependencies":"Pinned repaired source; theorem closure also uses prior B12 lines 403-683 coverage.","downstream":"No new obstruction."}
